"Multi-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreferencefor Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction (https://nlp.cs.washington.edu/sciIE/)\nciteturn27view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://nlp.cs.washington.edu/sciIE/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 23\nL0: ## Multi-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreferencefor Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction\nL1: \nL2: #### cite0†Yi Luan†ssli.ee.washington.edu , cite1†Luheng He†homes.cs.washington.edu , cite2†Mari Ostendorf†ssli.ee.washington.edu , cite3†Hannaneh Hajishirzi†homes.cs.washington.edu .\nL3: ### Abstract\nL4: We introduce a multi-task setup of identifying entities, relations, and coreference clustersin scientific articles. We create SCIERC, adataset that includes annotations for all threetasks and develop a unified framework calledSCIIE with shared span representations. The multi-task setup reduces cascading errors between tasks and leverages cross-sentence relations through coreference links.\nL5: Experimentsshow that our multi-task model outperformsprevious models in scientific information extraction without using any domain-specific features. We further show that the framework supports construction of a scientific knowledgegraph, which we use to analyze information inscientific literature.\nL6: The details can be found in our paper:\nL7:   * cite4†Multi-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreferencefor Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction†arxiv.org L8: Yi Luan, Luheng He, Mari Ostendorf and Hannaneh Hajishirzi.\nL9: EMNLP, 2018 [cite5†bib ]\nL10: ### Datasets\nL11: Check out our cite6†raw dataset , our cite7†processed dataset (tokenized, in jason format, together with Elmo embeddings), and the cite8†annotation guideline .\nL12: Our dataset (called SCIERC) includes annotationsfor scientific entities, their relations, and coreference clusters for 500 scientific abstracts. These abstracts are taken from 12 AI conference/workshop proceedings in four AI communities, from the cite9†Semantic Scholar Corpus†labs.semanticscholar.org .\nL13: SCI-ERC extends previous datasets in scientific articles cite10†SemEval 2017 Task 10†scienceie.github.io and cite11†SemEval 2018 Task 7†competitions.codalab.org by extending entity types, relation types, relation coverage, and adding cross-sentence relations using coreference links.\nL14: An annotation example is as follows:\nL15: cite12†Image L16: ### Code\nL17: \nL18: Our method SciIE is an unified framework for identifying entities, relations, and coreference clusters in scientific articles with shared span representations. Check out our cite13†BitBucket Repository†bitbucket.org .\nL19: ### Application for Knowledge Graph Construction\nL20: With SciIE, we are able to extract entity, relation and coreference from large collection of scientific papers. We construct a scientific knowledge graph from a large corpus of scientific articles. The corpus includes all abstracts (110k in total) from 12 AIconference proceedings from the Semantic Scholar Corpus. Nodes in the knowledge graph correspond to scientific entities. Edges correspond to scientific relations between pairs of entities.\nL21: A part of an automatically constructed scientific knowledge graph is as follows:\nL22: cite14†Image --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMulti-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreference for Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction (https://aclanthology.org/D18-1360.pdf)\nciteturn27view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aclanthology.org/D18-1360.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1018\nL0@P0: Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 3219–3232\nL1@P0: Brussels, Belgium, October 31 - November 4, 2018.\nL2@P0: c 2018 Association for Computational Linguistics\nL3@P0: 3219\nL4@P0: Multi-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreference\nL5@P0: for Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction\nL6@P0: Yi Luan Luheng He Mari Ostendorf Hannaneh Hajishirzi\nL7@P0: University of Washington\nL8@P0: {luanyi, luheng, ostendor, hannaneh}@uw.edu\nL9@P0: Abstract\nL10@P0: We introduce a multi-task setup of identifying\nL11@P0: and classifying entities, relations, and coref\u0002erence clusters in scientific articles. We cre\u0002ate SCIERC, a dataset that includes annota\u0002tions for all three tasks and develop a uni\u0002fied framework called Scientific Information\nL12@P0: Extractor (SCIIE) for with shared span rep\u0002resentations. The multi-task setup reduces\nL13@P0: cascading errors between tasks and leverages\nL14@P0: cross-sentence relations through coreference\nL15@P0: links. Experiments show that our multi-task\nL16@P0: model outperforms previous models in scien\u0002tific information extraction without using any\nL17@P0: domain-specific features. We further show that\nL18@P0: the framework supports construction of a sci\u0002entific knowledge graph, which we use to ana\u0002lyze information in scientific literature.1\nL19@P0: 1 Introduction\nL20@P0: As scientific communities grow and evolve, new\nL21@P0: tasks, methods, and datasets are introduced and\nL22@P0: different methods are compared with each other.\nL23@P0: Despite advances in search engines, it is still hard\nL24@P0: to identify new technologies and their relationships\nL25@P0: with what existed before. To help researchers more\nL26@P0: quickly identify opportunities for new combina\u0002tions of tasks, methods and data, it is important to\nL27@P0: design intelligent algorithms that can extract and\nL28@P0: organize scientific information from a large collec\u0002tion of documents.\nL29@P0: Organizing scientific information into structured\nL30@P0: knowledge bases requires information extraction\nL31@P0: (IE) about scientific entities and their relationships.\nL32@P0: However, the challenges associated with scientific\nL33@P0: IE are greater than for a general domain. First, an\u0002notation of scientific text requires domain expertise\nL34@P0: which makes annotation costly and limits resources.\nL35@P0: 1Data and code are publicly available at: http://nlp.\nL36@P0: cs.washington.edu/sciIE/\nL37@P0: Figure 1: Example annotation: phrases that refer to\nL38@P0: the same scientific concept are annotated into the\nL39@P0: same coreference cluster, such as MORphological\nL40@P0: PAser MORPA, it and MORPA (marked as red).\nL41@P0: In addition, most relation extraction systems are de\u0002signed for within-sentence relations. However, ex\u0002tracting information from scientific articles requires\nL42@P0: extracting relations across sentences. Figure 1 il\u0002lustrates this problem. The cross-sentence relations\nL43@P0: between some entities can only be connected by\nL44@P0: entities that refer to the same scientific concept,\nL45@P0: including generic terms (such as the pronoun it,\nL46@P0: or phrases like our method) that are not informa\u0002tive by themselves. With co-reference, context-free\nL47@P0: grammar can be connected to MORPA through the\nL48@P0: intermediate co-referred pronoun it. Applying ex\u0002isting IE systems to this data, without co-reference,\nL49@P0: will result in much lower relation coverage (and a\nL50@P0: sparse knowledge base).\nL51@P0: In this paper, we develop a unified learning\nL52@P0: model for extracting scientific entities, relations,\nL53@P0: and coreference resolution. This is different from\nL54@P0: previous work (Luan et al., 2017b; Gupta and Man\u0002ning, 2011; Tsai et al., 2013; Gabor et al. ´ , 2018)\nL55@P0-1: which often addresses these tasks as independent3220\nL56@P1: components of a pipeline. Our unified model is\nL57@P1: a multi-task setup that shares parameters across\nL58@P1: low-level tasks, making predictions by leveraging\nL59@P1: context across the document through coreference\nL60@P1: links. Specifically, we extend prior work for learn\u0002ing span representations and coreference resolution\nL61@P1: (Lee et al., 2017; He et al., 2018). Different from a\nL62@P1: standard tagging system, our system enumerates all\nL63@P1: possible spans during decoding and can effectively\nL64@P1: detect overlapped spans. It avoids cascading errors\nL65@P1: between tasks by jointly modeling all spans and\nL66@P1: span-span relations.\nL67@P1: To explore this problem, we create a dataset SCI\u0002ERC for scientific information extraction, which\nL68@P1: includes annotations of scientific terms, relation\nL69@P1: categories and co-reference links. Our experiments\nL70@P1: show that the unified model is better at predict\u0002ing span boundaries, and it outperforms previous\nL71@P1: state-of-the-art scientific IE systems on entity and\nL72@P1: relation extraction (Luan et al., 2017b; Augenstein\nL73@P1: et al., 2017). In addition, we build a scientific\nL74@P1: knowledge graph integrating terms and relations\nL75@P1: extracted from each article. Human evaluation\nL76@P1: shows that propagating coreference can signifi\u0002cantly improve the quality of the automatic con\u0002structed knowledge graph.\nL77@P1: In summary we make the following contribu\u0002tions. We create a dataset for scientific information\nL78@P1: extraction by jointly annotating scientific entities,\nL79@P1: relations, and coreference links. Extending a previ\u0002ous end-to-end coreference resolution system, we\nL80@P1: develop a multi-task learning framework that can\nL81@P1: detect scientific entities, relations, and coreference\nL82@P1: clusters without hand-engineered features. We use\nL83@P1: our unified framework to build a scientific knowl\u0002edge graph from a large collection of documents\nL84@P1: and analyze information in scientific literature.\nL85@P1: 2 Related Work\nL86@P1: There has been growing interest in research on au\u0002tomatic methods for information extraction from\nL87@P1: scientific articles. Past research in scientific IE\nL88@P1: addressed analyzing citations (Athar and Teufel,\nL89@P1: 2012b,a; Kas, 2011; Gabor et al., 2016; Sim et al.,\nL90@P1: 2012; Do et al., 2013; Jaidka et al., 2014; Abu\u0002Jbara and Radev, 2011), analyzing research com\u0002munity (Vogel and Jurafsky, 2012; Anderson et al.,\nL91@P1: 2012), and unsupervised methods for extracting sci\u0002entific entities and relations (Gupta and Manning,\nL92@P1: 2011; Tsai et al., 2013; Gabor et al. ´ , 2016).\nL93@P1: More recently, two datasets in SemEval 2017\nL94@P1: and 2018 have been introduced, which facilitate\nL95@P1: research on supervised and semi-supervised learn\u0002ing for scientific information extraction. SemEval\nL96@P1: 17 (Augenstein et al., 2017) includes 500 para\u0002graphs from articles in the domains of computer\nL97@P1: science, physics, and material science. It includes\nL98@P1: three types of entities (called keyphrases): Tasks,\nL99@P1: Methods, and Materials and two relation types:\nL100@P1: hyponym-of and synonym-of. SemEval 18 (Gabor ´\nL101@P1: et al., 2018) is focused on predicting relations be\u0002tween entities within a sentence. It consists of six\nL102@P1: relation types. Using these datasets, neural mod\u0002els (Ammar et al., 2017, 2018; Luan et al., 2017b;\nL103@P1: Augenstein and Søgaard, 2017) are introduced for\nL104@P1: extracting scientific information. We extend these\nL105@P1: datasets by increasing relation coverage, adding\nL106@P1: cross-sentence coreference linking, and removing\nL107@P1: some annotation constraints. Different from most\nL108@P1: previous IE systems for scientific literature and gen\u0002eral domains (Miwa and Bansal, 2016; Xu et al.,\nL109@P1: 2016; Peng et al., 2017; Quirk and Poon, 2017;\nL110@P1: Luan et al., 2018; Adel and Schutze ¨ , 2017), which\nL111@P1: use preprocessed syntactic, discourse or corefer\u0002ence features as input, our unified framework does\nL112@P1: not rely on any pipeline processing and is able to\nL113@P1: model overlapping spans.\nL114@P1: While Singh et al. (2013) show improvements\nL115@P1: by jointly modeling entities, relations, and coref\u0002erence links, most recent neural models for these\nL116@P1: tasks focus on single tasks (Clark and Manning,\nL117@P1: 2016; Wiseman et al., 2016; Lee et al., 2017; Lam\u0002ple et al., 2016; Peng et al., 2017) or joint entity\nL118@P1: and relation extraction (Katiyar and Cardie, 2017;\nL119@P1: Zhang et al., 2017; Adel and Schutze ¨ , 2017; Zheng\nL120@P1: et al., 2017). Among those studies, many papers as\u0002sume the entity boundaries are given, such as (Clark\nL121@P1: and Manning, 2016), Adel and Schutze ¨ (2017) and\nL122@P1: Peng et al. (2017). Our work relaxes this constraint\nL123@P1: and predicts entity boundaries by optimizing over\nL124@P1: all possible spans. Our model draws from recent\nL125@P1: end-to-end span-based models for coreference res\u0002olution (Lee et al., 2017, 2018) and semantic role\nL126@P1: labeling (He et al., 2018) and extends them for the\nL127@P1: multi-task framework involving the three tasks of\nL128@P1: identification of entity, relation and coreference.\nL129@P1: Neural multi-task learning has been applied to\nL130@P1: a range of NLP tasks. Most of these models share\nL131@P1: word-level representations (Collobert and Weston,\nL132@P1: 2008; Klerke et al., 2016; Luan et al., 2016, 2017a;\nL133@P1: Rei, 2017), while Peng et al. (2017) uses high-order\nL134@P1-2: cross-task factors. Our model instead propagates3221\nL135@P2: cross-task information via span representations,\nL136@P2: which is related to Swayamdipta et al. (2017).\nL137@P2: 3 Dataset\nL138@P2: Our dataset (called SCIERC) includes annotations\nL139@P2: for scientific entities, their relations, and corefer\u0002ence clusters for 500 scientific abstracts. These ab\u0002stracts are taken from 12 AI conference/workshop\nL140@P2: proceedings in four AI communities from the Se\u0002mantic Scholar Corpus2\nL141@P2: . SCIERC extends pre\u0002vious datasets in scientific articles SemEval 2017\nL142@P2: Task 10 (SemEval 17) (Augenstein et al., 2017) and\nL143@P2: SemEval 2018 Task 7 (SemEval 18) (Gabor et al. ´ ,\nL144@P2: 2018) by extending entity types, relation types, rela\u0002tion coverage, and adding cross-sentence relations\nL145@P2: using coreference links. Our dataset is publicly\nL146@P2: available at: http://nlp.cs.washington.\nL147@P2: edu/sciIE/. Table 1 shows the statistics of SCI\u0002ERC.\nL148@P2: Annotation Scheme We define six types for an\u0002notating scientific entities (Task, Method, Metric,\nL149@P2: Material, Other-ScientificTerm and Generic) and\nL150@P2: seven relation types (Compare, Part-of, Conjunc\u0002tion, Evaluate-for, Feature-of, Used-for, Hyponym\u0002Of). Directionality is taken into account except\nL151@P2: for the two symmetric relation types (Conjunction\nL152@P2: and Compare). Coreference links are annotated\nL153@P2: between identical scientific entities. A Generic en\u0002tity is annotated only when the entity is involved\nL154@P2: in a relation or is coreferred with another entity.\nL155@P2: Annotation guidelines can be found in Appendix A.\nL156@P2: Figure 1 shows an annotated example.\nL157@P2: Following annotation guidelines from Qasem\u0002iZadeh and Schumann (2016) and using the BRAT\nL158@P2: interface (Stenetorp et al., 2012), our annotators\nL159@P2: perform a greedy annotation for spans and always\nL160@P2: prefer the longer span whenever ambiguity occurs.\nL161@P2: Nested spans are allowed when a subspan has a\nL162@P2: relation/coreference link with another term outside\nL163@P2: the span.\nL164@P2: Human Agreements One domain expert anno\u0002tated all the documents in the dataset; 12% of the\nL165@P2: data is dually annotated by 4 other domain experts\nL166@P2: to evaluate the user agreements. The kappa score\nL167@P2: for annotating entities is 76.9%, relation extraction\nL168@P2: is 67.8% and coreference is 63.8%.\nL169@P2: 2These conferences include general AI (AAAI, IJCAI),\nL170@P2: NLP (ACL, EMNLP, IJCNLP), speech (ICASSP, Interspeech),\nL171@P2: machine learning (NIPS, ICML), and computer vision (CVPR,\nL172@P2: ICCV, ECCV) at http://labs.semanticscholar.\nL173@P2: org/corpus/\nL174@P2: Statistics SCIERC SemEval 17 SemEval 18\nL175@P2: #Entities 8089 9946 7483\nL176@P2: #Relations 4716 672 1595\nL177@P2: #Relations/Doc 9.4 1.3 3.2\nL178@P2: #Coref links 2752 - -\nL179@P2: #Coref clusters 1023 - -\nL180@P2: Table 1: Dataset statistics for our dataset SCIERC\nL181@P2: and two previous datasets on scientific information\nL182@P2: extraction. All datasets annotate 500 documents.\nL183@P2: Comparison with previous datasets SCIERC\nL184@P2: is focused on annotating cross-sentence relations\nL185@P2: and has more relation coverage than SemEval 17\nL186@P2: and SemEval 18, as shown in Table 1. SemEval 17\nL187@P2: is mostly designed for entity recognition and only\nL188@P2: covers two relation types. The task in SemEval 18\nL189@P2: is to classify a relation between a pair of entities\nL190@P2: given entity boundaries, but only intra-sentence re\u0002lations are annotated and each entity only appears\nL191@P2: in one relation, resulting in sparser relation cover\u0002age than our dataset (3.2 vs. 9.4 relations per ab\u0002stract). SCIERC extends these datasets by adding\nL192@P2: more relation types and coreference clusters, which\nL193@P2: allows representing cross-sentence relations, and\nL194@P2: removing annotation constraints. Table 1 gives a\nL195@P2: comparison of statistics among the three datasets.\nL196@P2: In addition, SCIERC aims at including broader\nL197@P2: coverage of general AI communities.\nL198@P2: 4 Model\nL199@P2: We develop a unified framework (called SCIIE)\nL200@P2: to identify and classify scientific entities, relations,\nL201@P2: and coreference resolution across sentences. SCIIE\nL202@P2: is a multi-task learning setup that extends previous\nL203@P2: span-based models for coreference resolution (Lee\nL204@P2: et al., 2017) and semantic role labeling (He et al.,\nL205@P2: 2018). All three tasks of entity recognition, re\u0002lation extraction, and coreference resolution are\nL206@P2: treated as multinomial classification problems with\nL207@P2: shared span representations. SCIIE benefits from\nL208@P2: expressive contextualized span representations as\nL209@P2: classifier features. By sharing span representations,\nL210@P2: sentence-level tasks can benefit from information\nL211@P2: propagated from coreference resolution across sen\u0002tences, without increasing the complexity of infer\u0002ence. Figure 2 shows a high-level overview of the\nL212@P2: SCIIE multi-task framework.\nL213@P2: 4.1 Problem Definition\nL214@P2: The input is a document represented as a sequence\nL215@P2-3: of words D = {w1, . . . , wn}, from which we de\u0002rive S = {s1, . . . , sN }, the set of all possible3222\nL216@P3: MORPA\nL217@P3: MORPA is a\nL218@P3: a fully implemented parser\nL219@P3: parser\nL220@P3: MORphological Parser MORPA\nL221@P3: …, the MORphological Parser MORPA is provided with a … MORPA is a fully implemented parser developed for …\nL222@P3: MORphological\nL223@P3: MORPA is\nL224@P3: is provided with\nL225@P3: NULL\nL226@P3: Hyponym-of Used-for NULL\nL227@P3: Task Method NULL\nL228@P3: Sentences\nL229@P3: BiLSTM outputs\nL230@P3: Span\nL231@P3: Representations\nL232@P3: Entity\nL233@P3: Recognition\nL234@P3: +Span Features\nL235@P3: Coreference\nL236@P3: Resolution\nL237@P3: Relation\nL238@P3: Extraction\nL239@P3: … …\nL240@P3: MORPA parser\nL241@P3: MORphological\nL242@P3: MORphological Parser MORPA\nL243@P3: …\nL244@P3: … …\nL245@P3: Figure 2: Overview of the multitask setup, where all three tasks are treated as classification problems on\nL246@P3: top of shared span representations. Dotted arcs indicate the normalization space for each task.\nL247@P3: within-sentence word sequence spans (up to a rea\u0002sonable length) in the document. The output con\u0002tains three structures: the entity types E for all\nL248@P3: spans S, the relations R for all pair of spans S × S,\nL249@P3: and the coreference links C for all spans in S. The\nL250@P3: output structures are represented with a set of dis\u0002crete random variables indexed by spans or pairs\nL251@P3: of spans. Specifically, the output structures are\nL252@P3: defined as follows.\nL253@P3: Entity recognition is to predict the best entity type\nL254@P3: for every candidate span. Let LE represent the set\nL255@P3: of all possible entity types including the null-type \u000f.\nL256@P3: The output structure E is a set of random variables\nL257@P3: indexed by spans: ei ∈ LE for i = 1, . . . , N.\nL258@P3: Relation extraction is to predict the best relation\nL259@P3: type given an ordered pair of spans (si, sj ). Let LR\nL260@P3: be the set of all possible relation types including\nL261@P3: the null-type \u000f. The output structure R is a set of\nL262@P3: random variables indexed over pairs of spans (i, j)\nL263@P3: that belong to the same sentence: rij ∈ LR for\nL264@P3: i, j = 1, . . . , N.\nL265@P3: Coreference resolution is to predict the best an\u0002tecedent (including a special null antecedent) given\nL266@P3: a span, which is the same mention-ranking model\nL267@P3: used in Lee et al. (2017). The output structure\nL268@P3: C is a set of random variables defined as: ci ∈\nL269@P3: {1, . . . , i − 1, \u000f} for i = 1, . . . , N.\nL270@P3: 4.2 Model Definition\nL271@P3: We formulate the multi-task learning setup as\nL272@P3: learning the conditional probability distribution\nL273@P3: P(E, R, C|D). For efficient training and inference,\nL274@P3: we decompose P(E, R, C|D) assuming spans are\nL275@P3: conditionally independent given D:\nL276@P3: P(E, R, C | D) = P(E, R, C, S | D) (1)\nL277@P3: =\nL278@P3: Y\nL279@P3: N\nL280@P3: i=1\nL281@P3: P(ei| D)P(ci| D)\nL282@P3: Y\nL283@P3: N\nL284@P3: j=1\nL285@P3: P(rij | D),\nL286@P3: where the conditional probabilities of each random\nL287@P3: variable are independently normalized:\nL288@P3: P(ei = e | D) = exp(ΦE(e, si))\nL289@P3: P\nL290@P3: e\nL291@P3: 0∈LE\nL292@P3: exp(ΦE(e\nL293@P3: 0\nL294@P3: , si)) (2)\nL295@P3: P(rij = r | D) = exp(ΦR(r, si\nL296@P3: , sj ))\nL297@P3: P\nL298@P3: r\nL299@P3: 0∈LR\nL300@P3: exp(ΦR(r\nL301@P3: 0\nL302@P3: , si, sj ))\nL303@P3: P(ci = j | D) = exp(ΦC(si\nL304@P3: , sj ))\nL305@P3: P\nL306@P3: j\nL307@P3: 0∈{1,...,i−1,\u000f}\nL308@P3: exp(ΦC(si, sj\nL309@P3: 0)),\nL310@P3: where ΦE denotes the unnormalized model score\nL311@P3: for an entity type e and a span si, ΦR denotes the\nL312@P3: score for a relation type r and span pairs si, sj ,\nL313@P3: and ΦC denotes the score for a binary coreference\nL314@P3: link between si and sj . These Φ scores are further\nL315@P3: decomposed into span and pairwise span scores\nL316@P3: computed from feed-forward networks, as will be\nL317@P3: explained in Section 4.3.\nL318@P3: For simplicity, we omit D from the Φ functions\nL319@P3: and S from the observation.\nL320@P3: Objective Given a set of all documents D, the\nL321@P3: model loss function is defined as a weighted sum of\nL322@P3: the negative log-likelihood loss of all three tasks:\nL323@P3: −\nL324@P3: X\nL325@P3: (D,R∗,E∗,C∗)∈D\nL326@P3: n\nL327@P3: λE log P(E\nL328@P3: ∗\nL329@P3: | D) (3)\nL330@P3: + λR log P(R\nL331@P3: ∗\nL332@P3: | D) + λC log P(C\nL333@P3: ∗\nL334@P3: | D)\nL335@P3-4: o3223\nL336@P4: where E∗, R∗, and C\nL337@P4: ∗\nL338@P4: are gold structures of the en\u0002tity types, relations, and coreference, respectively.\nL339@P4: The task weights λE, λR, and λC are introduced as\nL340@P4: hyper-parameters to control the importance of each\nL341@P4: task.\nL342@P4: For entity recognition and relation extraction,\nL343@P4: P(E∗| D) and P(R∗| D) are computed with\nL344@P4: the definition in Equation (2). For coreference\nL345@P4: resolution, we use the marginalized loss follow\u0002ing Lee et al. (2017) since each mention can have\nL346@P4: multiple correct antecedents. Let C\nL347@P4: ∗\nL348@P4: i\nL349@P4: be the set\nL350@P4: of all correct antecedents for span i, we have:\nL351@P4: log P(C\nL352@P4: ∗\nL353@P4: | D) = P\nL354@P4: i=1..N log Pc∈C∗\nL355@P4: i\nL356@P4: P(c | D).\nL357@P4: 4.3 Scoring Architecture\nL358@P4: We use feedforward neural networks (FFNNs) over\nL359@P4: shared span representations g to compute a set\nL360@P4: of span and pairwise span scores. For the span\nL361@P4: scores, φe(si) measures how likely a span si has\nL362@P4: an entity type e, and φmr(si) and φmc(si) measure\nL363@P4: how likely a span siis a mention in a relation or a\nL364@P4: coreference link, respectively. The pairwise scores\nL365@P4: φr(si, sj ) and φc(si, sj ) measure how likely two\nL366@P4: spans are associated in a relation r or a coreference\nL367@P4: link, respectively. Let gi be the fixed-length vec\u0002tor representation for span si\nL368@P4: . For different tasks,\nL369@P4: the span scores φx(si) for x ∈ {e, mc, mr} and\nL370@P4: pairwise span scores φy(si, sj ) for y ∈ {r, c} are\nL371@P4: computed as follows:\nL372@P4: φx(si) =wx · FFNNx(gi)\nL373@P4: φy(si, sj ) =wy · FFNNy([gi, gj , gi\nL374@P4:  gj ]),\nL375@P4: where\nL376@P4:  is element-wise multiplication, and\nL377@P4: {wx, wy} are neural network parameters to be\nL378@P4: learned.\nL379@P4: We use these scores to compute the different Φ:\nL380@P4: ΦE(e, si) = φe(si) (4)\nL381@P4: ΦR(r, si, sj ) = φmr(si) + φmr(sj ) + φr(si, sj )\nL382@P4: ΦC(si, sj ) = φmc(si) + φmc(sj ) + φc(si, sj )\nL383@P4: The scores in Equation (4) are defined for entity\nL384@P4: types, relations, and antecedents that are not the\nL385@P4: null-type \u000f. Scores involving the null label are\nL386@P4: set to a constant 0: ΦE(\u000f, si) = ΦR(\u000f, si, sj ) =\nL387@P4: ΦC(si, \u000f) = 0.\nL388@P4: We use the same span representations g from\nL389@P4: (Lee et al., 2017) and share them across the three\nL390@P4: tasks. We start by building bi-directional LSTMs\nL391@P4: (Hochreiter and Schmidhuber, 1997) from word,\nL392@P4: character and ELMo (Peters et al., 2018) embed\u0002dings.\nL393@P4: For a span si, its vector representation giis con\u0002structed by concatenating si’s left and right end\nL394@P4: points from the BiLSTM outputs, an attention\u0002based soft “headword,” and embedded span width\nL395@P4: features. Hyperparameters and other implementa\u0002tion details will be described in Section 6.\nL396@P4: 4.4 Inference and Pruning\nL397@P4: Following previous work, we use beam pruning to\nL398@P4: reduce the number of pairwise span factors from\nL399@P4: O(n\nL400@P4: 4\nL401@P4: ) to O(n\nL402@P4: 2\nL403@P4: ) at both training and test time,\n"
